d"]},"sid":["itu-6705e66e-0e21-4895-9792-688433450537"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Ghana upgraded its census to make it more inclusive: but old tensions still surfaced","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-689186","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"This paper explores how biometric payment practices in Ghana reinforce historically grown, patriarchal frames of representing women’s economic participation. As central modes of participation in social life, payment practices tie closely into national narratives of modernity and economic development. In this light, Ghana’s current biometric identification agenda, of which biometric payment constitutes a central element, has come to associate female entrepreneurs with normative gender identities which question their discipline, autonomy, and meaningful contribution to the economy. Two different institutions seeking to classify Ghanaian women in the interest of altering their behavior will be at the heart of the discussion. In the first case, I will focus on development experts’ routine representation of (Ghanaian) women as “dependent” on assistance. In the second case, I focus on the regulatory institutions of Ghana’s financial sector and the pursuit to formalize what is generally framed as “deviant” female entrepreneurship. The article addresses how the articulation of these normative gender identities reproduces historically rooted conceptions of Ghanaian women’s autonomy and economic participation, to the effect of their continued exclusion from relevant public decision-making processes. The paper finally explores the unconventional ways in which female Ghanaian entrepreneurs-turned-activists index emancipatory gender identities to seek recognition for their economic contribution.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Thiel, Alena"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-689194"],"filters":{"added":"2023-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Ghana","Human kinds","biometricidentiﬁcation technologies","normative gender identities","ﬁnancial inclusion"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"biometric payment and gendered kinds in ghana","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/25729861.2021.1924486","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-689194","ids":["itu-abf19fcc-8a0f-4a9d-bbce-2b8cce886112"],"keyword":["Ghana","biometricidentiﬁcation technologies","human kinds","normative gender identities","ﬁnancial inclusion"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-81827AC1EA08077B32261036C0D5B8F3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/25729861.2021.1924486","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Alena","key":"thielpau","last":"Thiel","locid":"f240d002-893f-45bd-b9ad-6a6ddc015e40","name":"Thiel, Alena","orcid":"0000-0002-1092-1140","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["25729861"],"issue":"1","journal":"Tapuya: Latine American Science, Technology and Society","paperid":"1924486","status":"p","title":"Tapuya: Latine American Science, Technology and Society","volume":"4"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-abf19fcc-8a0f-4a9d-bbce-2b8cce886112"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Biometric payment and gendered kinds in Ghana","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-689194","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"In the global effort to strengthen national identification systems (SDG 16.9), biometric identification technologies and civil registration systems have been associated with different motives and applications, thus fuelling their competition for public attention and resources. The case of Ghana illustrates how these alternative systems, along with further sources of personal data, have recently been integrated into the larger political vision of a centralised, national population data system. Based on ethnographic fieldwork, the paper traces the difficulties and institutional negotiations that accompany this integration into a centralised population data infrastructure. Acknowledging how sets of actors, infrastructures and power relations are layered onto each other to unintended effects, the article describes the historical process of institutional and infrastructural harmonisation in the production of biometric population registers in Ghana.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Thiel, Alena"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-689193"],"filters":{"added":"2023-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Biometrics","Data revolution","Ghana","Ghanacard","national identification system"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"biometric identification technologies and the ghanaian data revolution","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S0022278X19000600","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-689193","identifier":[{"id":"85079751419","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-867dd83b-111e-4571-84af-9cd06da8a336"],"keyword":["Ghana","Ghanacard","biometrics","data revolution","national identification system"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E95E92312E18D21C1C69F4B3118A54B5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S0022278X19000600","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":1,"key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU"}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Alena","key":"thielpau","last":"Thiel","locid":"f240d002-893f-45bd-b9ad-6a6ddc015e40","name":"Thiel, Alena","orcid":"0000-0002-1092-1140","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1017/s0022278x19000600","issn":["0022278X","14697777"],"issue":"1","journal":"The Journal of Modern African Studies","pages":"115-136","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","status":"p","title":"The Journal of Modern African Studies","volume":"58"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-867dd83b-111e-4571-84af-9cd06da8a336"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Biometric identification technologies and the Ghanaian ‘data revolution’","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-689193","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
